From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maago <maago@example.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:41:30 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Attach files to posts

Attach files to posts, as the original's uploads (fastapi-storages) did.

- The model Attachment: the post, the key, the name sent, the type, the
  size. They are the four fields of the maago.Media that
  maago.SaveMedia returns. The file is not in the row: it is in
  Config.MediaDir (media/ in main)
- The form AttachmentForm: one maago.Field[maago.File]. Without an
  accept= tag it takes any kind. The type comes from the start of the
  content, not from the name sent or what the browser says. A form with
  a file field comes only as multipart/form-data, and maago gen refuses
  a page that has no form with enctype="multipart/form-data"
- The upload, attachment_add, is the author's and staff's:

      func Attach(ctx context.Context, u auth.User, tx maago.Tx, in AttachIn) (maago.Result[PostPage], error)

  It returns the same PostPage as the post, so a form with an error
  draws again where it was. A body over Config.MaxBody (10MB by
  default) is a 413
- The download, attachment, is a func(w, r) view. No route serves the
  media directory as it is: the view reads the post, says 404 for a
  hidden one, lets only the author and staff have a draft's, and then
  calls maago.ServeMedia. It is always a download (attachment), so an
  uploaded HTML file never opens as a page of the site. An escape view
  takes no parameters, so it reads the user from the request, as it
  does the database: maago.UserFrom[auth.User](ctx)

The file's name is a random key maago makes. A name sent with ../ in it
writes nothing outside the media directory: the name sent never becomes
a path. The row keeps it for the download, without its directories (Go's
multipart drops them).

Nothing goes on a hidden post either (404).

Deleting a post without comments deletes its attachments' rows first
(they point at the post: a foreign key), and their files once the
transaction has committed (maago.AfterCommit): a rollback keeps the rows
and their files. A file that will not go is logged and left (the post
is gone already). A post with comments is hidden, and its attachments
with it.

Attach saves the file inside the request's transaction, which stays
open meanwhile (on SQLite, holding its write lock). When writing the row fails, the file
goes. When the commit fails after it, the file stays with no row
pointing at it, as do files whose rows the admin deleted: clearing them
is beyond this tutorial.

media/.gitignore keeps the directory in the repository: Handler stops
rather than make a MediaDir that is not there.

